{"5bd447d4e2fa4531b99e9ad16eaff169": {"id": "5bd447d4e2fa4531b99e9ad16eaff169", "title": "Uganda Overnight And Global", "started": 1792137563.3785403, "updated": 1792137563.3785403, "turns": [{"q": "Give me a briefing on Uganda first, then the world: what happened overnight and what matters today.", "a": "**The gist:** Kampala's night was quiet — the news is a court blow to the Besigye prosecution and a vote recount running today; abroad, it was a heavy night, with a US school shooting and a missile strike on Yemen's presidential palace. **Uganda** - **Court rejects digital evidence in Besigye case.** High Court Justice Emmanuel Baguma declined to admit a flash disk of audio and video recordings allegedly tying Kizza Besigye and his aide Lutale to the treason file, ruling the witness who produced it laid no foundation. The disk stays on the record for identification only. Filed 6:37 AM. - **UK investors in Kampala.** A delegation is scouting opportunities here; reported 7:08 AM, early detail thin. - **Kassanda South recount runs today.** Ballot boxes were delivered to Mubende High Court under heavy security for the recount Justice Mary Babirye ordered on a petition by independent candidate Eria Mubiru. Security presence is the story as much as the count. - **Moneylender confesses to ritual manslaughter,** and separately **three KCCA officials were remanded over alleged Munyonyo land fraud** — both fresh this morning. - **C-Care completes its buy of Roswell Hospital.** Announced Thursday: 30 beds added, taking the group to six hospitals and 17 clinics; the deal was signed 8 September. A consolidation signal in Kampala private health. - **Maria Prean, founder of Vision for Africa, has died** — carried this morning. **World** - **Hormuz shipping attacks hit a record high.** Reported attacks on vessels near the Strait ran at their highest level yet as oil exports slow. This is the one with a price tag attached for the region. - **Oregon school shooting.** Children aged 5, 8 and 11 were killed; the suspect was shot by a bystander. 6:45 AM. - **Houthi missile strikes Yemen's presidential palace** — five dead, a minister says. 2:38 AM. - **US teen's plot on a Hindu temple foiled** after a tip-off; **suspicious-substance letters sent to US senators and election offices**; and a **bus hit a train stopped on the French side of the Channel Tunnel, injuring 19** — all overnight. - **Trump called off the livestream of the Fort Hood attacker's firing-squad execution** last night. Read from Nile Post, New Vision, Monitor and the BBC world wire between 10:58 and 11:05 AM today, 16 October. Times are Kampala; anything older than the overnight drop I left out. The Uganda line worth your attention is Kassanda South — a recount today, boxes already in court, is where local trouble usually starts.", "t": 1792137563.3785403, "ms": 75385, "s": [{"label": "Read one of its own skills"}, {"label": "Read the clock"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}, {"label": "Ran cd"}]}], "named": true}, "bb315ff49bd34dd89234b9a60f92a3c9": {"id": "bb315ff49bd34dd89234b9a60f92a3c9", "title": "What is the news in Uganda today, and why does it matter?", "started": 1791986515.0, "updated": 1791986514.7427385, "named": false, "turns": [{"q": "What is the news in Uganda today, and why does it matter?", "a": "Uganda's central bank raised its benchmark interest rate to 12% today at an emergency meeting to arrest the shilling's collapse — the biggest hike since 2011 and the highest rate in nine years for the country. The move came after the shilling hit 4,143 to the dollar, a 33-year low, and inflation turned upward. Measure | Now | Was\nBank rate | 12.0% | 9.75%\nShilling | 4,143 | 3,920\nHeadline CPI | 4.6% | 4.1%\nCore CPI | 3.7% | 3.5%\nCurrent acct | $4.6bn | $3.2bn Shilling and current-account rows are per US dollar and in deficit terms. Source: Bank of Uganda MPC statement and press coverage, 13-14 Oct 2026 (ChimpReports, Pulse, Trading Economics). **Why it matters** This is a cost-of-living story before it is a markets story. The weaker shilling makes every imported barrel of fuel dearer, and fuel is what sets the price of transport, food and everything moved by road. The bank is now forecasting headline inflation of 7-7.5% over the next year, up from 4.6% — a near-doubling of the pace at which your money loses value. For you specifically, it cuts two ways on the car work: pump prices are the distance-based fuel line in the hire rate card, so a drifting shilling pushes your cost per trip up and the open quote for upcountry jobs becomes riskier; and any borrowing you hold or take on now prices against a 12% policy rate, so credit is materially more expensive than it was last week. **The rest of today's headlines** - **Fuel:** a global diesel crunch from Strait of Hormuz disruption is adding to pump-price pressure locally\n- **WiFi crackdown:** the communications regulator gave ~4,000 unlicensed public WiFi operators seven days to shut down\n- **Charcoal ban:** enforcement intensified, with the energy regulator pushing connected households to switch to electricity\n- **Investment:** Uganda offered Thai investors 10-year tax holidays and full foreign ownership to court Southeast Asian capital\n- **Namayingo:** closure of illegal gold sites left about 500 miners stranded\n- **Courts:** George Musisi declared duly elected Kira Municipality MP", "t": 1791986514.7427385}]}, "7b9b832354614b6c9a1696aeaa868666": {"id": "7b9b832354614b6c9a1696aeaa868666", "title": "Compare Bolt and Faras for a driver in Kampala: what each takes off the fare, an", "started": 1792078859.0, "updated": 1792079832.0, "named": false, "turns": [{"q": "Compare Bolt and Faras for a driver in Kampala: what each takes off the fare, and which leaves more in a day. Keep it short.", "a": "Faras keeps more of each fare, Bolt sends more fares — on equal takings Faras wins. On your own logged trips, Faras took ten percent and Bolt took seventeen point three, so a hundred thousand shillings of fares leaves you about ninety thousand on Faras against about eighty-two thousand seven hundred on Bolt. Bolt only beats that when its extra volume closes the roughly seven-point gap.", "t": 1791621851.0}, {"q": "In two or three sentences, why does a shell script start with a shebang, and when does it not need one?", "a": "The shebang tells the kernel which interpreter to hand the file to, so it works even when you run it as ./script from a shell that would otherwise guess, or from cron where there is no shell to guess at all. Skip it only when you always launch the script through the interpreter explicitly, as in bash script.sh, or when a fixed interpreter is guaranteed, since the kernel falls back to sh or gives you a syntax error otherwise.", "t": 1791622290.0}, {"q": "What does a shebang do in a shell script?", "a": "It names the interpreter. ### Notes\n\n- one\n- two", "t": 1791623385.0}, {"q": "first question about the disk", "a": "### First answer\n\n- one\n- two", "t": 1791623436.0}, {"q": "second question about the tyre", "a": "The second answer, in prose.", "t": 1791623436.0}, {"q": "third question about the fuel", "a": "The third answer, in prose.", "t": 1791623437.0}, {"q": "Where do the controls sit on this turn?", "a": "An answer with a little body to it. ### Part\n\n- one\n- two", "t": 1791623951.0}, {"q": "Measure the anchors on a settled turn", "a": "An answer body long enough that the footer sits below it. ### Part\n\n- one\n- two", "t": 1791623985.0}, {"q": "Compare my two income lines at a glance, the Bolt driving and the car hire desk, and tell me which one carries more.", "a": "Per job the hire desk carries far more, but on money actually banked neither line has earned yet. The driving has 55,000 logged over two days and sits 165,612 down once the police fine is counted; the desk has set prices of 120,000 a half day and 200,000 a full day with no platform